<h2>Introduction</h2>

<p>In April 2026, the Mercator Institute for China Studies (MERICS) published an in-depth report titled <em>Embodied AI: China's Ambitious Path to Transform Its Robotics Industry</em>. The report compares China's humanoid robot industry strategy with that of the United States.</p>

<p>Meanwhile, Brookings has also published comparative reports on US-China AI strategies. TIME magazine ran a more direct headline: <em>China Could Dominate the Physical AI Future</em>. Newsweek warned that America's AI blind spots are being exposed by China.</p>

<p>These threads of information together paint the competitive landscape of the humanoid robotics industry in 2026.</p>

<hr />

<h2>The &quot;Two Paths&quot; of the US and China</h2>

<p>A core analytical framework of the MERICS report is that the US and China have adopted fundamentally different strategic approaches in humanoid robotics.</p>

<h3>China's Path: Industrial Scale-Up + Government Guidance</h3>
<p>- <strong>Core characteristic</strong>: Entry from the manufacturing end, leveraging existing supply chains for rapid scaling</p>
<p>- <strong>Capital profile</strong>: National-level guiding funds (~138 billion yuan) + local government subsidies</p>
<p>- <strong>Industry goal</strong>: Integrate humanoid robots into the broader manufacturing upgrade plan</p>
<p>- <strong>Representative cases</strong>: Beijing Half Marathon humanoid robots, X Square Robot's push into household scenarios</p>

<h3>America's Path: Frontier Exploration + Capital-Driven</h3>
<p>- <strong>Core characteristic</strong>: Entry from foundational technology R&D, focusing on algorithms and AI model innovation</p>
<p>- <strong>Capital profile</strong>: Venture capital-driven (global humanoid robot VC reached $40.7 billion in 2025)</p>
<p>- <strong>Industry goal</strong>: Build general-purpose capability platforms, solve technical problems before worrying about scale</p>
<p>- <strong>Representative cases</strong>: NVIDIA GTC 2026 emphasized AI Agent + Physical AI integration</p>

<p><strong>Neither path is inherently superior — they reflect different starting conditions and industrial strategies.</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2>Where Does China's Advantage in Humanoid Robots Lie?</h2>

<p>According to MERICS analysis, China's core competitive advantages include:</p>

<h3>1. Manufacturing Capability and Supply Chain Depth</h3>
<p>China has the world's largest robotics hardware supply chain. A WSJ report reveals a telling detail: some American robotics companies use high-end components sourced from Chinese suppliers. This means that at the hardware level, China is already a critical node in the global robotics industry.</p>

<h3>2. Policy Guidance and Industrial Coordination</h3>
<p>MERICS points out that China's model of &quot;national guiding funds + local government matching&quot; delivers unique capital efficiency. In a capital-intensive, long-cycle industry like robotics, this model creates a different resource allocation dynamic compared with purely market-driven approaches.</p>

<h3>3. Application Scenario Advantage</h3>
<p>China has the world's largest manufacturing base and dense population distribution — meaning abundant testing and deployment scenarios for humanoid robots.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Where Does the US Hold the Competitive Edge?</h2>

<p>Meanwhile, the US maintains clear advantages in the following areas:</p>

<h3>1. Foundational Research and Algorithm Innovation</h3>
<p>In areas such as AI foundation models, motion control algorithms, and multimodal perception, US research institutions and companies remain globally领先.</p>

<h3>2. Talent Ecosystem</h3>
<p>Although the talent gap is narrowing, the US still has the world's largest pool of AI and robotics researchers.</p>

<h3>3. Open-Source Ecosystem</h3>
<p>The dominant global robotics and AI development frameworks remain largely US-led open-source projects, which directly influence the direction of global tech community innovation.</p>

<hr />

<h2>From &quot;Competition&quot; to &quot;Complementarity&quot;</h2>

<p>The differences between the two paths make it easy to focus on the question of &quot;who is winning.&quot; But a more meaningful question is: can these differences become complementary?</p>

<p>In a globalized context, the technology R&D and supply chain of the humanoid robotics industry are inherently international — even if geopolitical factors add a layer of complexity and uncertainty.</p>

<p>TIME magazine's question — &quot;China Could Dominate the Physical AI Future&quot; — is a scenario worth considering, but it is not the only possibility. A more likely outcome is the parallel development of two ecosystems, each building advantages in different domains.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Implications for Enterprises</h2>

<h3>1. Focus on Supply Chain Flexibility</h3>
<p>Regardless of how US and China strategies evolve, diversifying and flexibly configuring the humanoid robot supply chain will become a source of competitive advantage for enterprises.</p>

<h3>2. Value International Cooperation</h3>
<p>Although geopolitics adds complexity to technology collaboration, the international division of labor in robotics hardware and software will not fundamentally change in the short term.</p>

<h3>3. Pragmatic Technology Choices</h3>
<p>For application-focused enterprises, &quot;whose robot is better&quot; matters less than &quot;whose robot fits my scenario better.&quot;</p>

<hr />

<h2>Conclusion</h2>

<p>The humanoid robotics industry is at a critical stage — moving from &quot;the lab to the factory floor.&quot; The US and China have adopted different industrial paths, each with its own strengths and limitations. For enterprises, understanding the differences and potential points of convergence between these two paths is key to finding their place in the evolving industry landscape.</p>

